    <Placemark>
      <name>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi (עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi (עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jordan</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2.3, 2.2 (Pages 45, 46, 48)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A complex water system including artificial channels (two long, two short) leading to &apos;wells&apos; (interpreted as reservoirs/basins) in a shallow depression. This system was designed to collect and store runoff water from a catchment area of about 29 dunams, potentially holding up to 13,900 cubic meters. A settlement located on the eastern outskirts of Amman, Jordan, near a spring.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Pre_Pottery_Neolithic</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Jordan</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-8300 to -5500 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.2.3, 2.2</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>45, 46, 48</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>A complex water system including artificial channels (two long, two short) leading to &apos;wells&apos; (interpreted as reservoirs/basins) in a shallow depression. This system was designed to collect and store runoff water from a catchment area of about 29 dunams, potentially holding up to 13,900 cubic meters. A settlement located on the eastern outskirts of Amman, Jordan, near a spring.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>36.833,31.883,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2 (Pages 45, 48)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A well, approximately 6 meters deep, demonstrating advanced hydrological and engineering knowledge, construction capabilities, and quarrying skills of the inhabitants. Another well at the site was only partially excavated.</p>]]></description>
